      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does a walking pad cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most walking pads cost between about $130 and $450. Basic flat pads start around $130 to $250, longer and sturdier pads from the better-known brands cost $250 to $450, and pads with incline, a handrail or a fold-up running mode range from about $160 to $800. On 21 November 2026, for example, WalkingPad's Z1 was $299 and UREVO's SpaceWalk E1L $132.99 on the makers' own stores.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much is a walking pad with incline?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually about $230 to $400. Incline adds surprisingly little at the walking pad end: WalkingPad's H1 Plus, with nine levels of powered incline, was $239 and UREVO's SpaceWalk 3S, with a 9% auto incline, $239.99 on the makers' stores in November 2026. Pads with steeper lifts, such as UREVO's CyberPad for Home at a listed 14%, cost more.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a $150 walking pad worth it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For walking by one person within its weight limit, often yes. A budget pad gives you the same basic job as a pricier one, usually with a shorter belt and fewer extras. It is a poor buy for tall walkers, heavy daily desk use or jogging, where a longer belt and sturdier build last longer and cost less per year.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why are some walking pads so expensive?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The top of the range buys a longer, wider deck, a higher top speed with a handrail or fold-up bar, and sometimes a higher weight rating. WalkingPad's R3 Hybrid+ at $759, for example, lists a 47.2 by 17.3-inch running area and up to 7.5 mph. Warranties are usually about a year across the range, so the extra money buys hardware, not longer cover.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a walking pad cheaper than a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Most walking pads cost $130 to $450, while a budget folding treadmill typically costs $400 to $900 and a running-ready mid-range treadmill $1,000 to $2,000. The pad is only cheaper to own if you use it for walking; used for regular running, it tends to wear out early.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What else do I need to buy with a walking pad?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A mat (about $40 to $65), a bottle of silicone lubricant (about $15 to $25) and, if you will walk while you work, a standing desk. The desk is the cost people forget: in WalkingPad's bundles it added roughly $130 to $170 to the price of a C2 in November 2026.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "When are walking pads cheapest?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Black Friday and Cyber Monday are usually the biggest sale of the year, with Prime Day and the new-year fitness season next. Many pads already sat well below their compare-at prices the week before Black Friday 2026, so judge any deal against the recent selling price rather than the list price.",
          },
