        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How much does a walking pad cost?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Most walking pads cost between about $130 and $450. Basic flat pads start around $130 to $250, longer and sturdier pads from the better-known brands cost $250 to $450, and pads with incline, a handrail or a fold-up running mode range from about $160 to $800. On 21 November 2026, for example, WalkingPad's Z1 was $299 and UREVO's SpaceWalk E1L $132.99 on the makers' own stores.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much is a walking pad with incline?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually about $230 to $400. Incline adds surprisingly little at the walking pad end: WalkingPad's H1 Plus, with nine levels of powered incline, was $239 and UREVO's SpaceWalk 3S, with a 9% auto incline, $239.99 on the makers' stores in November 2026. Pads with steeper lifts, such as UREVO's CyberPad for Home at a listed 14%, cost more.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a $150 walking pad worth it?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For walking by one person within its weight limit, often yes. A budget pad gives you the same basic job as a pricier one, usually with a shorter belt and fewer extras. It is a poor buy for tall walkers, heavy daily desk use or jogging, where a longer belt and sturdier build last longer and cost less per year.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why are some walking pads so expensive?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The top of the range buys a longer, wider deck, a higher top speed with a handrail or fold-up bar, and sometimes a higher weight rating. WalkingPad's R3 Hybrid+ at $759, for example, lists a 47.2 by 17.3-inch running area and up to 7.5 mph. Warranties are usually about a year across the range, so the extra money buys hardware, not longer cover.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a walking pad cheaper than a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Most walking pads cost $130 to $450, while a budget folding treadmill typically costs $400 to $900 and a running-ready mid-range treadmill $1,000 to $2,000. The pad is only cheaper to own if you use it for walking; used for regular running, it tends to wear out early.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What else do I need to buy with a walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A mat (about $40 to $65), a bottle of silicone lubricant (about $15 to $25) and, if you will walk while you work, a standing desk. The desk is the cost people forget: in WalkingPad's bundles it added roughly $130 to $170 to the price of a C2 in November 2026.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>When are walking pads cheapest?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Black Friday and Cyber Monday are usually the biggest sale of the year, with Prime Day and the new-year fitness season next. Many pads already sat well below their compare-at prices the week before Black Friday 2026, so judge any deal against the recent selling price rather than the list price.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
